- a way to define temporary variables for a proc (on the stack)?
- types must be defined before they are used
- .module or .namespace
- .obj, .objend
- .radix
- .type, .symtype
- .pushstate, .popstate
- .local
- variable-number-of-argument macros
- -W flag allowing more fine-grained control of warnings
- inlining function calls?
- a way to specify fixed-point numbers?
- .ifdef inside struct/union

Compatibility planning:

- Future listing and binary object formats may deliberately break compatibility
  when the benefits justify it; preserving existing formats is not an absolute
  constraint. Plan format/schema versioning where appropriate, migration notes,
  and coordinated updates to Nesrev and other consumers with regression coverage.
  Keep the current formats unchanged until such a change is designed and validated.
- Aim for one supported structured-listing schema, retaining a version identifier
  in its output rather than indefinite selectable legacy schemas. Before removing
  --listing-version, migrate Nesrev and other consumers and decide how object-mode
  listings should work (v2 currently supports pure binary only). Document the
  migration and validate consumers together; this plan changes no current format.

Known defects and follow-ups:

- Late word instruction operands outside -32768..65535 still warn and truncate
  in pure-binary output while the linker rejects them. Decide the policy together
  with 16-bit address wrapping: existing binaries/listings can cross $FFFF. Late
  byte operands now have matching assembler/linker range checks.
- FCEUX ROM export rejects an unevaluable or negative storage count during
  analysis collection, before the writer can supply its diagnostic listing.
  A late negative count can lose the located range error too, leaving only
  "could not collect assembly analysis" and exit 3 without a manifest. With a
  dependency manifest this also exits 3 when an invalid shift has already
  produced a located error. Omit ROM export to obtain the ordinary diagnostic.
  Deferring collection failure also needs safe destination planning: unknown
  size means not all ROM bank paths can be enumerated, and allowing a listing
  can overwrite an NL file that was omitted from the incomplete plan.
- An omitted structure-array count that still depends on $ after constant
  substitution uses first-element expansion and cannot become zero in
  pure-binary mode. Object output retains its previous link-time behavior.
  The binary remainder evaluates the count after the first element, changing
  some layouts from 1.9.2, which evaluated it at the field start. Constants
  and label differences support late zero counts, including nested layouts.
- Equates (.equ and =) fold $ during pass 1, before deferred storage sizes
  settle. They can capture a provisional PC, including after a late-zero
  omitted structure array, ordinary .dsb or a built-in array. PC-dependent
  counts defined this way no longer contain $ when later validation runs.
  Defer or reject these captures when layout is uncertain; meanwhile use a
  label for the final address, or define the count before allocating storage.
- Both outer omitted-array counts and nested field counts that depend on $
  can differ between binary and object output: binary uses the remainder's
  PC after the first element for an outer count and each field's PC for nested
  counts; object merging uses the start of the merged storage. At origin zero,
  an omitted Inner[3-$] with two-byte elements and a trailing byte occupies
  three bytes in binary, seven in object output and in 1.9.2. Keep address-
  dependent field counts in labels/constants when both output modes must agree.
- Conditional extents for late omitted structure-array counts add expression
  evaluation work, especially for nested arrays and object output. Benchmark
  omitted arrays too, not only explicitly initialized arrays, when reducing
  this overhead. Known positive counts use direct expansion.
- Shared alias chains and dependent scoped offsets that remain unresolved
  (for example, with label or external counts) are expanded as trees, so
  evaluation time and object size can grow exponentially. Completed integer
  values are cached per evaluation. Sufficiently deep definitions can also
  exhaust the recursive evaluator's stack.
- Repeated cyclic references within one ordinary expression can report once
  per use (e.g. .byte Value+Value), rather than once per expression.
- Invalid dotted storage counts can report the same error for both the count
  and byte-size expressions, followed by a range error. Cyclic counts in
  uninitialized structures can also report at both instance and declaration.
- Union dimensions must be known when the union is declared.
- A field remainder that becomes zero only at link time is encoded as storage
  and rejected by the linker instead of being omitted.
- A forward code-label difference used as storage size can assemble in binary
  mode but fail object link layout with "storage size must evaluate to an
  integer during layout". For example, .dsb Tail-Head before Head: nop / nop /
  Tail: rts emits two zero bytes in binary. Support resolvable forward
  differences during link layout. Listing v2 already describes the full fill
  span with byte_count and fill_byte; text/v1 intentionally omit storage
  bytes while retaining following addresses and output offsets.
- Data-segment storage counts built from data-segment labels can fail link
  layout with "storage size must evaluate to an integer during layout", even
  when the link script provides a RAM region.
- A late error in merged storage can point to the preceding storage statement
  instead of the statement that supplied the invalid count.
- Signed integer overflow in constant folding is undefined (e.g.
  .byte ($40000000+$40000000)>>31); sanitizer builds diagnose it.
- Forward strings differ between binary and object output, and exported forward
  constant aliases can still fail object encoding.
- Pure-binary address evaluation resolves LABEL symbols but not PROC or VAR
  symbols. Direct calls to procedures and operands using variable declarations
  can fail with "operand does not evaluate to literal" even though their objects
  link correctly. Explicit labels in procedure bodies work in both modes.
